    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sneferu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sneferu.jpg" alt="Sneferu" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sneferu_portrait,_Cairo_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Mortel, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a>, called Gubla by its own people and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> texts, Gebal in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> and Jbeil today, lies on a low headland on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> about 35 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. The site has been settled since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> times, and it is among the oldest continuously inhabited towns in the world. Its fortune rested on two assets: a small natural harbour and the forests of cedar, pine and fir on the slopes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-lebanon">Mount Lebanon</a> behind it, the best <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> within reach of treeless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. In the first half of the third millennium BCE a fishing and farming village grew into a walled town with stone houses and temples, and by about 2600 BCE it was a city with close ties to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> of the Old Kingdom of Egypt. Egyptian objects inscribed with royal names from the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-dynasty-of-egypt">Second Dynasty</a> onwards have been found in its temples, and Egyptian texts called large seagoing vessels ‘Byblos ships’, after the voyages that brought back timber and resin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo-stone">Palermo Stone</a> records that King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> received forty ships laden with cedar wood, probably from this coast. At the centre of the city stood the temple of its goddess, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a>, the Lady of Byblos, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/byblos"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byblos.jpg" alt="Byblos" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_Libanon_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Orient at de.wikipedia, CC BY-SA 2.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> lived by exchange. It sent cedar logs, resin, oils and wine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and received gold, alabaster vessels, linen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> in return; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, who obtained papyrus through the city, called the material <em>byblos</em>, from which come <em>biblion</em>, book, and ultimately the word Bible. Local farmers grew grain, olives and vines on the narrow coastal plain, while fishermen, shipbuilders, carpenters and metalworkers served the port. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> the city’s craftsmen produced fine jewellery, weapons and bronze figurines of gods, hundreds of which, some covered in gold leaf, were buried as offerings in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-obelisks">Temple of the Obelisks</a>. Religion centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a> and on male gods later identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a> and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osiris">Osiris</a>; according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarch">Plutarch</a>, the chest containing the body of Osiris washed ashore at Byblos. The city was also a laboratory of writing. Its scribes used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> and Babylonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>, and an undeciphered local system known as the Byblos syllabic script appears on stone and bronze inscriptions of the second millennium BCE. Around the end of the second millennium, inscriptions in the Phoenician alphabet appear at Byblos, among the earliest examples of the twenty-two-letter script from which the Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> alphabets descend.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii.jpg" alt="Thutmose III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luxor_Museum,_Egypt_49_(trim).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ولاء, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> was never a great military power. Its defences were its walls, rebuilt many times, and its ability to secure the protection of stronger states, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. A destruction by fire in the late third millennium BCE ended the prosperous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> city, though the cause is uncertain. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a>’s campaigns brought the Levantine coast under Egyptian control, Byblos served as a loyal base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>. Its vulnerability is vividly documented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> of the fourteenth century BCE: King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> wrote more than sixty letters to the pharaohs <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a>, pleading for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> and supplies as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdi-ashirta">Abdi-Ashirta</a> of Amurru and then his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aziru">Aziru</a> seized neighbouring towns, cut off the city’s grain supply and turned its own people against him. Help rarely came, and Rib-Hadda was eventually driven from his city. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, Byblos lacked the fleets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and Sidon and generally submitted to the empires that dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> rather than resisting them. Its ships nonetheless had military value: in 332 BCE its king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enylus">Enylus</a>, joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> with his squadron during the siege of Tyre after learning that Byblos had already submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonians</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun.jpg" alt="Story of Wenamun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenamun-papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ancient Egyptian Writer, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> was ruled throughout its history by kings who governed the city and its small hinterland under the patronage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baalat-gebal">Baalat Gebal</a>, and who presented themselves as her servants. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a> local rulers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abishemu-i">Abishemu</a> and his son Ipshemuabi adopted Egyptian forms, writing their names in hieroglyphs with the Egyptian title of a provincial governor and receiving gifts from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iv">Amenemhat IV</a>, objects found in their rock-cut tombs. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> the king was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> who reported to the pharaoh, but he also had to manage a council of leading citizens; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a>’s letters complain that his own nobles and townspeople were ready to abandon him. The Egyptian <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Report of Wenamun</a></em>, from about 1075 BCE, shows King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zakar-baal">Zakar-Baal</a> receiving an envoy of the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and demanding payment for cedar, consulting the records of his ancestors and treating Egypt as a trading partner rather than an overlord. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> inscriptions of kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yehimilk">Yehimilk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abibaal">Abibaal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elibaal">Elibaal</a> record the building and restoration of temples. Under Assyrian, Babylonian and Persian rule, the kings of Byblos kept their thrones as tributaries; the Persian-period stele of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yehawmilk">Yehawmilk</a> shows him standing before the enthroned goddess.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahiram"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahiram.jpg" alt="Ahiram" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahiram_sarcophag_from_Biblos_XIII-XBC.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matson Photo Service, photographer, published in 1936, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> was the main point of contact between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> for much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, and through its harbour flowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, ideas and artistic styles in both directions. Egyptian influence on its temples, tombs and royal titles, and Levantine influence in Egypt, made it a channel of exchange between two worlds over more than two thousand years. Its most lasting contribution is to writing. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a> sarcophagus, inscribed by Ahiram’s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ittobaal-of-byblos">Ittobaal</a> and usually dated to around 1000 BCE, though some scholars place it earlier, carries one of the oldest substantial texts in the Phoenician alphabet, and the royal inscriptions of tenth-century Byblos are basic sources for the early alphabet that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> adapted. The Greek name of the city became the word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and then for book. In later antiquity Byblos was celebrated as a centre of the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a>, and the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philo-of-byblos">Philo of Byblos</a>, in the Roman period, claimed to translate an ancient Phoenician history by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanchuniathon">Sanchuniathon</a>, a source on Phoenician religion. Modern excavation, begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-renan">Ernest Renan</a> in 1860 and continued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pierre-montet">Pierre Montet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-dunand">Maurice Dunand</a>, uncovered layers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">Crusades</a>, and the site was made a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1984.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i.jpg" alt="Shoshenq I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shoshenq_I_by_Rosellini.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ippolito Rosellini (1800-1843), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> declined slowly rather than collapsing. After the end of Egyptian rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> in the twelfth century BCE it survived the upheavals that destroyed cities such as Ugarit, and in the tenth century its kings were still prosperous enough to dedicate inscribed statues of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shoshenq-i">Shoshenq I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osorkon-i">Osorkon I</a> to their goddess. But in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, commercial and political leadership in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> and Sidon, whose fleets and colonies spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, while Byblos remained a smaller port. From the ninth century BCE it paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurnasirpal-ii">Ashurnasirpal II</a> received its gifts on his march to the Mediterranean, and later kings of Byblos appear in the tribute lists of Tiglath-Pileser III and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>. It passed under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a> and from 539 BCE under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, when it minted its own <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins showing warships and was home to a Persian-period fortress. In 332 BCE Byblos submitted peacefully to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, and its kings disappear from the record soon afterwards under Macedonian rule. The town continued under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a>, who built a castle there, and Jbeil remains a living town today.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>For over two thousand years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> linked the forests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> to the Egyptian court, absorbing Egyptian culture while its scribes experimented with writing; inscriptions from the city are among the earliest in the Phoenician alphabet, and its name gave Greek the word for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> and, through it, the word Bible.</em></p>

